<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hugo Grotius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1583">1583</a>–<a href="/years/1645">1645</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hugo Grotius was a seventeenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> jurist, a founder of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>. His works addressed the law of war and peace and freedom of the seas. The site notes that debates about the Spanish conquest and the rights of indigenous peoples, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>'s legacy, contributed to the international law developed by writers such as Grotius.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hugo-grotius.jpg" alt="Hugo Grotius" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michiel Jansz. van Mierevelt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michiel_Jansz_van_Mierevelt_-_Hugo_Grotius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hugo Grotius</b> wrote Mare Liberum (1609) in defence of the VOC, and it became a founding text of international law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hugo Grotius</b>’s Mare Liberum (1609) and De jure belli ac pacis (1625) laid foundations of modern international law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The debates about the legal and moral status of indigenous peoples prompted by the conquest contributed to the emergence of international law through the School of Salamanca, above all Francisco de Vitoria, whose ideas were later developed by <b>Hugo Grotius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch Republic, with about 1.5 million people, became a great power in the 17th century and controlled a worldwide network of trade; its relative religious toleration drew refugees, merchants and thinkers, and its Golden Age produced Rembrandt, Johannes Vermeer, <b>Hugo Grotius</b>, Christiaan Huygens and Antonie van Leeuwenhoek.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch lawyers such as <b>Hugo Grotius</b> led in international and commercial law, and Antonie van Leeuwenhoek was the first to describe bacteria.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li></ul></section>
